# Citation File Format 1.2.0 -- https://citation-file-format.github.io
#
# Why this file exists: the lab publishes measured numbers (I1 headroom, I2
# worst-case bounds, break timings) and teaching material that other people
# quote. A quote is only checkable if the reader can get back to the exact thing
# that produced it, so the repository says how it wants to be named rather than
# leaving each citer to invent one.
#
# `version` tracks package.json. There is no `date-released` or `commit` field
# because this lab has no tagged release yet -- adding either would be a claim
# about an artifact that does not exist. Cite the commit you actually read.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this demo, its figures, or its measured numbers, please cite it as
  below, and name the commit you read -- the numbers are regenerated, not
  hand-maintained.
title: "Crypto Lab: GGH Trapdoor"
abstract: >-
  An educational browser demo of the GGH lattice trapdoor. One lattice is shown
  under two bases -- a short, nearly orthogonal private basis R and a long,
  skewed public basis B = U*R -- and the demo proves they span the same lattice
  over the integers, displays the round-off decryption bound per ciphertext, and
  then runs the two historical attacks that ended the scheme: Nguyen's mod-2σ
  reduction (CRYPTO 1999) against GGH encryption, and the Nguyen–Regev
  learning-a-parallelepiped attack (EUROCRYPT 2006), a fourth-moment descent,
  against the GGH signature design. Every "attack succeeded" verdict is decided
  by the real decryptor or the real signature verifier rather than by comparison
  with the secret. This is a teaching implementation, not production
  cryptography.
type: software
authors:
